    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> in 1537, it kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> monarchy, court and Sun cult alive in the mountains of Vilcabamba until 1572.</li><li>Its rulers combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war with diplomacy, and Titu Cusi’s account of 1570 gave an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> view of the Spanish conquest.</li><li>Viceroy Toledo’s army took Vilcabamba in 1572, and the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> ended independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-bingham-iii">Hiram Bingham III</a> reached the ruins of Vilcabamba at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a> in 1911, but believed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>, which he had also explored that year, was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> refuge.</p></aside>
